Revision history for Geo-Coder-Free

0.49	Tue Oct 20 08:03:03 PM EDT 2026
	[ Enhancements ]
	- Requires Perl 5.20, which Geo::Coder::Free::Utils has always needed
	- Module::Runtime, Try::Tiny and namespace::clean are runtime prerequisites;
	  Error, JSON::PP and Test::Exception are test prerequisites
	- The modules the website needs are listed as the optional 'website' feature,
	  and Geo::libpostal and Test::Consumer::Contract 0.04 are recommended
	[ Bug Fixes ]
	- Imported functions such as croak() can no longer be called as methods of
	  Geo::Coder::Free, its backends or Geo::Coder::Free::Display::query
	- Fixed a POD error in Geo::Coder::Free::OpenAddresses
	- Geo::Coder::Free::MissCache->expires_after() now says it wants a whole
	  number of seconds
	[ Documentation ]
	- Documented Geo::Coder::Free::MissCache in full, and added API specifications
	  for clear_miss_cache() and provenance()
	[ Tests ]
	- t/consumer-contract.t needs Test::Consumer::Contract 0.04 and skips, rather
	  than failing, with an older one, which failed the install outside a
	  workspace of sibling checkouts by trying to read
	  Geo-Location-Point/lib/Geo/Location/Point.pm

0.48	Mon Oct 19 07:35:53 PM EDT 2026
	[ Enhancements ]
	- The location rule has moved to a distribution of its own, Geo-Location-Predicate
	  (Geo::Location::Predicate::_valid_location()), so the sibling geocoders can use it
	  without depending on Geo-Coder-Free.  Its answers are unchanged
	- Requires Geo-Location-Predicate 0.01 and Geo::Location::Point 0.19
	- Makefile.PL installs a missing configure-time prerequisite correctly on Windows
	- Added Geo::Coder::Free::MissCache, replacing the never-expiring miss hash in
	  OpenAddresses.  OpenAddresses, Local and MaxMind all use it, so an address that is
	  not in the data is searched for once per process rather than once per call.
	  Entries expire after an hour (expires_after()), and
	  Geo::Coder::Free->clear_miss_cache() empties the cache or one backend's part of it
	- Each backend has reads(), the number of reads it has made.  geocode_many()'s
	  lookups now counts only values that caused a read, and a new reads key gives the
	  reads made by each backend
	- The field-to-accuracy rule has moved from Geo::Coder::Free::Provenance to
	  Geo::Location::Point::accuracy_for().  Provenance still exports ACCURACY_LEVELS
	- cgi-bin/page.fcgi now validates a CSRF token when a client sends one, as documented
	[ Tests ]
	- t/location-rule.t also scans the six sibling geocoders for copies of the location rule
	- Added scripts/location-predicate-fuzz.pl, which compares the sibling geocoders'
	  verdicts with the rule, and t/location-predicates.t and t/location-fuzz-doc.t,
	  which test it and LOCATION-PREDICATE-FUZZ.md
	- Added .github/workflows/location-predicates.yml and geocoder-suites.yml, which run
	  the fuzz and the sibling geocoders' test suites against this distribution

0.47	Fri Oct 16 09:34:18 PM EDT 2026
	[ Enhancements ]
	- Added geocode_many(), the loop a batch caller writes around geocode()
	  It takes an arrayref of locations and answers with one entry
	  per distinct value - the point it resolved to, or undef - plus lookups (how many values were
	  looked up), resolved and unresolved (which add up to the number of distinct values) and
	  unresolved_values (the values that produced nothing, in the order they were first seen, which
	  is what a caller records as tried-and-failed instead of retrying them on every request).  A
	  value repeated in the batch is one lookup, so a column holding the same handful of places
	  thousands of times costs the handful; a value the module refuses before any backend is touched
	  - a bare number, the empty string, undef, a reference - costs no lookup at all and is counted
	  unresolved, so the count a caller caps its work by is the number of backends asked.  An undef
	  is the empty string and shares its entry; region and on_error are handed to every lookup and
	  mean there what they mean in geocode(), croak by default; and a backend exception is still an
	  exception.  The four keys the answer carries besides the values are reserved, and a value
	  spelled like one is refused by name rather than overwriting a count.  A batch of no values
	  answers with counts of zero
	- The token filter in _find_word_ngrams() asks the shared location rule what a bare number
	  is instead of spelling the rule out again with its own regex.  The answer is the same for
	  every token that can reach it - the text is trimmed and its whitespace collapsed before the
	  split, so no empty token can arise - and the rule is now answered in one place (below)
	- The databases Makefile.PL downloads are fetched with LWP::Simple again, and LWP::Simple::WithCache is
	  no longer used to fetch them.  That module swaps in a caching user agent whose request($request,
	  $file) answers a cache hit, or a 304, straight out of the cache and never writes $file, so
	  getstore() answered 200 and left nothing behind: on any machine whose cache had been warmed by an
	  earlier install no database was fetched at all, while perl Makefile.PL reported "Looks good" and
	  make succeeded.  The failure arrived much later, as "gunzip: cities.csv.gz: No such file or
	  directory" from createdatabase.PL and a failing t/locales.t, so an install that should have worked
	  did not work rather than losing a feature.  HTTP::Cache::Transparent, which caches the file it is
	  asked for, is still used when it is installed
	- Makefile.PL checks the files it downloaded rather than the status codes: after wait_all_children()
	  every destination is looked for in the parent, because a download that fails in a forked child dies
	  with that child and the parent never saw it - which is how the missing databases went unnoticed.  A
	  download that didn't arrive is named by URL and file and stops the build, under AUTOMATED_TESTING
	  it is a warning instead, since a smoker cannot fix the network and the tests that need those files
	  skip, and a build with NO_NETWORK_TESTING says that it is fetching nothing rather than doing it
	  quietly
	- createdatabase.PL no longer runs bin/create_sqlite when cities.csv.gz is absent.  db2sql gunzips
	  that file, so without it the step could only print the gzip error and blame sqlite3 ("perhaps you
	  don't have sqlite3 installed") for a file that was never downloaded; it now names the file that is
	  missing and carries on, cities.sql being optional
	[ Tests ]
	- t/unit.t: the API ledger gains the geocode_many states (the map and the counts, the
	  distinct-value reduction, the region pass-through, the default croak and its message, an undef
	  cell, the reserved key, an empty batch, both calling conventions, the usage errors and a
	  backend that dies), and a subtest drives them against a backend that is asked once per call and
	  answers per location
	- t/edge_cases.t: section 9d runs a 401-row column holding four distinct values to hold "one
	  lookup per distinct value" to two backend calls rather than 397, and a column of nothing but
	  unusable values to zero lookups at all - the backend is mocked to die if it is called for one
	- t/location-rule.t: the location rule is written out in one file and no other.  It scans every
	  module under lib/, every script under bin/ and every program under cgi-bin/ for the rule's
	  question as source text - a negated digit class or an anchored all-digits test - and fails
	  naming the file, the line, the sub and the spelling, so a renamed copy of _valid_location(),
	  or the guard typed straight into a caller, is caught as well as a second copy of the sub.
	  POD, comments and anything after __END__/__DATA__ are masked, since prose that describes the
	  rule is not a copy of it, and regexes that merely resemble it (a postcode, a coordinate,
	  /^\d/, /\d+=\d+/) are not reported.  The scan is run first on source whose answer is known,
	  so it is shown to tell the rule from code that merely uses digits.  Sites that ask one of
	  those questions about something that is not a location - the CGI test number, a cache
	  configuration value - are listed with their reason, and an exemption that no longer names a
	  site fails too, so the table cannot outlive the code it was written for
	- t/vwf-sync.t says what the sync will do with each block of local code instead of only that it
	  is not the master's.  A block is a run of consecutive local lines; each line is compared with
	  the master's lines in the same passage on the words it holds (identifiers and numbers,
	  punctuation and whitespace ignored), and a line sharing at least half its words with one of
	  them is that line in an older revision, which the sync will replace - the master's revision is
	  printed beside ours - while a line sharing that much with none of them is code the master has
	  never had, which the sync will discard.  A line that could be either is reported as local code,
	  the reading that sends the reader to look at their own code, and each file's report ends with
	  the two totals.  The tracked files are unchanged and still fail the test; the difference is in
	  the report, where Display.pm's local %blacklist hash now stands apart from the die() beside it
	  (the master's croak() in an older revision) and Utils.pm's @EXPORT line is shown with the
	  master's shorter list rather than as an addition of ours.  The comparison is run on source
	  whose answer is known as well - a changed line beside an added subroutine, a reworded line, and
	  a local line whose neighbour is an unrelated upstream change - so a report that has lost the
	  distinction fails there first
	- t/locales.t: the geographic subtests skip when the MaxMind databases are absent instead of failing.
	  Those lookups need admin1.db and admin2.db, which Makefile.PL downloads, and a build that was never
	  given them cannot answer the calls at all - the backend croaks "Can't find a file called 'admin1'"
	  - so a machine with OPENADDR_HOME set and no downloaded data to go with it failed five subtests,
	  and with them the install.  The skip names the files that are missing, as t/maxmind.t names the
	  database it needs
	- Added t/downloads.t: the fetch path in Makefile.PL is held to asking LWP::Simple, to waiting for the
	  children that do the downloading and to looking for the file that arrived rather than at the status
	  code, since a download that doesn't happen is invisible in the build and turns up later as a test
	  failing for want of a database
	- t/bin.t drives the CGI's index and meta_data pages, not just the query page, and checks what they
	  render rather than only the coordinates the query page resolves.  The landing page is asked for the
	  title and description its stub configuration gives it and for the form that starts a search; the
	  status page for the site title it was configured with, the domain it was answered for and the
	  figures it counts.  Those figures are the log the pages keep - one entry per visit, and a page
	  records the visit it serves - so the test writes a log of its own making (CGI::Info::logdir()
	  honours LOGDIR) and asserts the counts rendered from it, the four visitor types at two web visits,
	  one mobile, three search engines and no robots, header row and all.  The domain is named
	  (SERVER_NAME) rather than taken from the host, and the log the status page counts is written after
	  the landing page has been served, since serving it is itself a visit.  Both pages render templates
	  this distribution ships - the query page's own template is not one of them, query.txt ships empty,
	  which is why its answer is only in the log - so their output is the page, and it is asserted as
	  such.  bin/testcgibin gained tests 5 and 6 to drive them
	- t/bin.t asserts the site's answer to a client it will not serve, and its answer to one it will.
	  The same query is asked twice, from the same address and with the same everything else, by a
	  client whose user agent Geo::Coder::Free::Allow blacklists (bin/testcgibin test 7) and by one
	  whose agent is an ordinary browser's (test 8): the first is answered with a 403 and the
	  plain-text block page and is not given the position, the second is served the position and gets
	  no block page.  The refusal is read back out of the access log as well - the row the page
	  writes for it carries the reason the user-agent blacklist gave ('zgrab is blacklisted'), which
	  no other layer of the page's access control produces, plus the 403 and the address that was
	  refused - so what is asserted is that layer refusing the client and not the account being
	  written by something else in the stack.  The log every run writes is written into the test's
	  own directory now (LOGDIR, for all the runs rather than only those that read it back) and the
	  page's temporary directory is the test's too (TMPDIR), so the throttle that counts requests is
	  this run's rather than one that carries the count over from the last, and no run leaves an
	  access log or a throttle outside the temporary directory.  The file also points its diagnostics
	  at STDOUT: Log::WarnDie, which the pages need installed, takes STDERR over, and a test's
	  diagnostics go there by default, so before this every failure in t/bin.t was reported as a test
	  name with no detail to say what the page had answered instead

0.46	Thu Oct 15 09:06:24 PM EDT 2026
	[ Enhancements ]
	- Added valid_location(), which answers whether a string is a location at all: the rule geocode()
	  applies before it touches a backend, made public.  A caller working down a spreadsheet column can
	  skip a row whose value is a bare number, or empty, instead of catching the croak that would
	  otherwise end the run.  The regex it replaces was private to this module, so no caller could
	  pre-filter with the module's own rule
	- geocode() takes on_error => 'croak' (the default, and the behaviour every existing caller already
	  has) or 'undef'.  'undef' returns undef - an empty list in list context - for the two documented
	  input errors, invalid_location and invalid_scantext, and for nothing else: a backend that throws
	  still throws, because nothing is wrapped in an eval, and a call with neither a location nor a
	  scantext is still a usage croak.  An on_error with any other value is refused by name rather than
	  ignored, so a caller who misspells it does not ship a batch job that dies on its first bad row
	- Geo::Coder::Free::Provenance reads the accuracy vocabulary from Geo::Location::Point instead of
	  keeping a second copy of it.  The levels a backend reports and the levels a point accepts are now
	  one list - Geo::Location::Point::ACCURACY_LEVELS(), re-exported as Provenance's ACCURACY_LEVELS -
	  so a level added to one cannot go missing from the other, and the prerequisite is Geo::Location::Point
	  0.18, which is the version that defines it.  t/accuracy-vocabulary.t asserts the two names are the
	  same list, that a point accepts every level in it and refuses one outside it, and that every level
	  _accuracy_for() can derive from the fields a backend matches on is one of them
	[ Tests ]
	- t/consumer-contract.t: the declaration now accounts for every accessor Geo::Location::Point
	  documents, and the checker (Test-Consumer-Contract 0.02) fails when the value object gains an
	  accessor that is in none of the declaration's lists.  geocoder, database, confidence and
	  accuracy are named as accessors a consumer may read off a result, country, city and state as
	  documented accessors only some results carry, and new, AUTOLOAD and ACCURACY_LEVELS as
	  documented but not slots of one result.  The four provenance accessors and the three attribute
	  accessors had been absent from the declaration since they were added to the value object, which
	  is the drift the new direction of the check is for

0.45	Thu Oct 15 01:23:24 PM EDT 2026
	[ Enhancements ]
	- Every Geo::Location::Point this distribution returns now carries geocoder, database, confidence and
	  accuracy, so a caller can tell which distribution and
	  which backend produced it, how good the match was and how precise the coordinate is.  geocoder is new
	  and additive, and the existing database values are unchanged for the consumers that match on them
	- Local, MaxMind and OpenAddresses each describe the match they actually made: an OpenAddresses geohash
	  hit is an address and a hand-curated entry a locality, a Local match is graded from the columns that
	  matched rather than the ones the call site offered, and a MaxMind cities row is a city or a region
	- Added provenance(), which returns the distribution, backend, confidence and accuracy of a result, as a
	  list or, in scalar context, as a hashref.  The backends' two spellings of database are reduced to one
	  vocabulary there.  It returns undef rather than guessing for something with no provenance to report,
	  such as a plain hash handed on by another geocoder
	- Added Geo::Coder::Free::Provenance: the GEOCODER constant, the ACCURACY_LEVELS vocabulary and
	  _accuracy_for(), so a backend describes a match without repeating the vocabulary.  They live in a
	  module of their own because Geo::Coder::Free::Utils is synced from VWF master, where a geocoder
	  result has no provenance - an addition there would not survive the next sync
	- Requires Geo::Location::Point 0.16, which stores and returns false values: a confidence of 0 or an
	  empty accuracy used to be discarded on write, so provenance could not have been reported honestly
	[ Tests ]
	- t/unit.t: the API ledger gains the provenance states, and a new section runs the real MaxMind backend
	  (the mock is restored first) to assert that a hit carries all four keys with an accuracy of city or
	  region - a point the test built itself would prove nothing about the stamping
	- t/integration.t: the OpenAddresses path is now asserted end to end - the database lookup is stubbed at
	  the seam _get() uses, so the stamping, the Storable round trip through the result cache and the thawed
	  second lookup are all the code under test
	- t/consumer-contract.t: geocoder, database, confidence and accuracy are declared as guaranteed producer
	  keys, so a backend that stops setting one of them fails here
	- t/bin.t: the skip for a run without OPENADDR_HOME covers the 26 tests the plan declares, not 28, so a
	  configuration that skips the whole file no longer ends with 'Bad plan' and a non-zero exit
	- t/unit.t needs neither the downloaded databases nor an optional address parser: the MaxMind section
	  answers the database lookups itself, Geo::libpostal and Geo::Address::Parser are hidden so that what a
	  location string parses to cannot decide whether a documented state is reachable, and the column scan
	  is driven with the components a dataset row really carries.  Both of those were passing on a machine
	  with the data and the parsers installed and failing in CI, which has neither

0.44	Thu Oct 15 10:22:42 AM EDT 2026
	[ Tests ]
	- Use Test::Log::Abstraction
	- Added t/consumer-contract.t: the reader/producer contract for the Geo::Location::Point this dist returns. It checks that each backend still sets
	  the keys the contract claims, and that every registered consumer in the workspace reads only the keys every backend guarantees - reading a key that
	  only one backend sets is undef for the others, which is how a caller silently takes the wrong branch. A consumer that is not registered fails the
	  discovery test, so the check cannot go stale

0.43	Fri Aug 14 08:24:12 PM EDT 2026
	[ Enhancements ]
	- _normalize rewritten with split to eliminate O(N³) catastrophic backtracking from (.+)\s+(.+)\s+(.+)
	- _find_gb_addresses commas now mandatory between parts to prevent exponential backtracking on comma-free text
	- _find_us_addresses and _find_ca_addresses converted all capturing groups to non-capturing (only $& used)
	- _find_us_addresses and _find_ca_addresses street name pattern bounded to prevent lazy-quantifier O(N²) expansion
	- _normalize_args now croaks on odd-count argument lists longer than 1 (closes logic gap M7)
	- wantarray tautologies in reverse_geocode eliminated (M8)
	- scantext ignore_words passed by reference instead of copying the full hash each call
	- %admin2cache_rev added as inverse of %admin2cache, replacing O(N) value scan with O(1) lookup in _prepare
	[ Tests ]
	- Added new tests
	  t/logic-reducer.t: new test file with partition-based proofs for all M-series reductions
	  t/unit.t: new black-box unit test file covering every public API of Geo::Coder::Free, Local, OpenAddresses, and Utils; uses Test::Mockingbird to mock MaxMind, Test::Returns for schema validation, and an API ledger to guarantee all documented states are exercised
	  t/integration.t: new end-to-end integration test file covering cross-module workflows; tests backend delegation chain, alternatives singleton, concurrency isolation, clone shallow-copy semantics, scantext memoisation, round-trip geocode/reverse, distance calculations, OA known_locations, MaxMind admin cache invariants, optional Geo::libpostal dep, and USA/US normalisation
	  t/edge_cases.t: new destructive/boundary/security test file; covers distance() boundary coordinates, undef/non-numeric/out-of-range inputs, cache hostile configs, purely numeric geocode rejection (M7 regression), SQL/shell/newline injection payloads, global variable isolation, and ReDoS regression tests for _normalize(), _find_gb_addresses(), and the OA city-name pattern
	  t/path.t: new CFG path-coverage test file; maps every unique execution path in GCF::new/geocode/reverse_geocode, Local::geocode/reverse_geocode/_search/_find_geographic_centres/_to_two_letter_state, and the shared helpers _normalize_args/_normalize/_abbreviate/_find_word_ngrams; 97 subtests covering all dispatch branches, guard exits, confidence levels, and mock-contamination hygiene
	  t/transaction.t: new transaction-flow/lifecycle test file; 18 subtests covering Local cache write-through, geocode idempotency, scantext miss memoization, OA exception propagation (no silent swallow), OA→Local delegation chain, all-miss recovery, one-shot __DATA__ lifecycle, alternatives map re-geocode cycle, geocode→reverse round-trip, USA/US normalization idempotency, lazy backend creation, clone shallow-copy state boundary (shared references visible across both sides), and object usability after mid-chain failure

